23:29:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes, I’m sure — and I understand why it feels counterintuitive. Let me explain why this isn’t a mistake but a deliberate, industry-wide safety standard that almost all serious antivirus products follow. The mental model you’re using is: **“It detected a threat, therefore it’s dangerous, so logically it should delete it.”** The real model the software has to use is: **“It detected something that *matches patterns* of a threat, but it could be wrong — so it must act safely and reversibly.”** Here’s what makes that critical: ### 1. The “detection” is never 100% certain — it’s a verdict from clues Antivirus software doesn’t have a magic “this is definitely evil” sensor. It uses signatures, heuristics, behavior monitoring, and machine learning to estimate: *“This looks extremely suspicious.”* But even a 99.9% certainty still leaves a sliver of doubt. Many detections — especially “generic” or behavior-based ones — are essentially saying: *“This acts like malware, but it could also be a poorly written legitimate tool.”* Instant deletion would gamble your valuable data on that sliver of doubt. ### 2. History is full of catastrophic false positives where deletion would have been a disaster In the real world, antivirus engines have mistakenly flagged critical system files, drivers, and even Windows updates as viruses. For example, in 2010, McAfee flagged a legitimate Windows XP system file `svchost.exe` as malware, causing mass system crashes. If that file had been *deleted* instead of quarantined, thousands of computers would have been permanently bricked; because it was only quarantined (or just blocked), recovery was possible. In 2022, several security suites flagged parts of Google Chrome or even Microsoft Office as threats — again, deletion would have wrecked those apps. Quarantine saved the day. ### 3. Quarantine *is* immediate removal from the system When a file is quarantined, it is moved out of its original location, encrypted, and rendered completely unable to execute. The threat is **neutralized instantly**. So from a security standpoint, there is zero benefit to permanent deletion — the threat can’t do anything anymore. The only difference is whether you retain the ability to undo a mistake. ### 4. You can always delete later — but you can never un-delete If after review you’re certain it’s malware, you can open quarantine and click “Delete” in one second. The outcome is identical to an auto-delete, but without the risk. The only “cost” is a few seconds of your time and a bit of disk space for the vault — a tiny price for preventing irreversible damage. ### 5. It’s the default for a reason, not because engineers “missed” something Antivirus vendors have spent decades refining this. They know that automatically shredding files would generate far more user anger and actual system damage than the temporary inconvenience of quarantining. Many do offer a setting for “automatic removal” if you really want it, but it’s usually hidden behind warnings precisely because it’s unsafe. So yes, I’m completely sure: quarantining instead of deleting is a core feature, not a flaw. It’s the antivirus equivalent of “innocent until proven guilty” — it isolates the suspect to protect you, but saves the evidence in case the accusation was wrong. That’s the smarter, safer approach every time.
